WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.180
Locks protect critical sections where shared state must be read or changed consistently.

2
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:14.040
A critical section is the part of the code where the application needs exclusive access to a resource or invariant.

3
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.380
The lock does not make the code faster.

4
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.960
It makes a specific region of code run with a specific access rule.

5
00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.160
The C# lock statement is a common way to provide exclusive access.

6
00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:30.780
Traditional lock behavior is based on Monitor, which enters and exits a protected region.

7
00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:39.920
Newer C# and .NET versions may also provide System.Threading.Lock for clearer synchronization in codebases that target those runtimes.

8
00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:46.900
The important design point is still the same: protect the state with a clear private synchronization object.

9
00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:52.060
Lock only on private or internal lock objects that the owning code controls.

10
00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:58.960
Avoid locking on public objects, strings, Type objects, or objects that external code can also lock.

11
00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:08.340
If unrelated code can acquire the same lock without coordination, the application can become difficult to reason about and may block in surprising places.

12
00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:11.100
Keep lock scopes small and reviewable.

13
00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:16.880
A lock should cover the state transition that needs protection, not an entire workflow by habit.

14
00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:25.700
Avoid slow I/O, network calls, database calls, logging-heavy work, user callbacks, or arbitrary event handlers while holding a lock.

15
00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:32.200
The longer a lock is held, the more likely it is to create contention or unexpected dependency chains.

16
00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:41.520
SemaphoreSlim can limit concurrency in asynchronous-friendly workflows, such as allowing only a certain number of operations to use a limited resource at once.

17
00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:47.140
It still requires careful release behavior, usually with a try-finally pattern around the protected work.

18
00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:53.320
A leaked semaphore slot can quietly reduce capacity until the application appears stuck.

19
00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:58.160
Reader-writer locks can help some read-heavy scenarios, but they add complexity.

20
00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:04.820
They require careful understanding of read locks, write locks, upgrades, fairness, and contention.

21
00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:09.480
Use them only when the read-heavy design is clear and measurement supports the complexity.

22
00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:15.760
Choose the simplest synchronization primitive that preserves correctness and can be reviewed.

23
00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:23.460
A clear lock around a small invariant is often better than a clever mix of primitives that few developers can safely modify later.
